Q: What is the N+1 problem in JPA/Hibernate? How do you fix it?

────────────────────────────────────────
👨‍🏫 What is N+1?

ORM loads a list (1 query), then for each row lazily loads a relation (N queries).
Total = **1 + N** queries.

Example:
List<Student> students = studentRepository.findAll(); // 1 query

for (Student s : students) {
    System.out.println(s.getCourses()); // N extra queries if courses is LAZY
}

100 students → ~101 queries → slow DB load, gets worse as data grows.

────────────────────────────────────────
Why it’s bad
- Extra round-trips to DB
- Latency grows with list size
- Common in APIs that serialize nested relations

────────────────────────────────────────
How to fix (keep associations LAZY by default)

Default best practice:
@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)   // prefer this
Don’t use EAGER globally — it loads relations even when not needed.

Then fetch on purpose only when required:

────────────────────────────────────────
Solution 1: JOIN FETCH (JPQL)

@Query("SELECT DISTINCT s FROM Student s JOIN FETCH s.courses")
List<Student> findAllWithCourses();

→ related data loaded in one (or few) SQL joins.
Use DISTINCT with OneToMany to avoid duplicate parent rows from the join.

────────────────────────────────────────
Solution 2: @EntityGraph (clean & recommended)

@OneToMany(mappedBy = "department")
private List<Employee> employees; // LAZY

public interface DepartmentRepository extends JpaRepository<Department, Long> {

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = "employees")
    List<Department> findAll();

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = "employees")
    Optional<Department> findWithEmployeesById(Long id);
}

Normal findById → department only.
Graph method → department + employees in one plan (join).

────────────────────────────────────────
Solution 3: DTO / constructor projection (great for APIs)

@Query("""
SELECT new com.example.dto.EmployeeDTO(e.name, d.name)
FROM Employee e JOIN e.department d
""")
List<EmployeeDTO> findEmployeeDTOs();

No entity graph walking → no lazy loads → no N+1.
Return only fields the API needs.

────────────────────────────────────────
Solution 4: @BatchSize (reduce, not always eliminate)

@BatchSize(size = 25)
on collection/entity → Hibernate loads lazy collections in batches
(e.g. 100 parents → fewer queries than 100, not always 1).

Useful mitigation; JOIN FETCH / EntityGraph / DTO still cleaner when you know you need the data.

────────────────────────────────────────
EAGER vs EntityGraph (interview point)

EAGER on @OneToMany:
- always loads employees, even when not needed
- can hurt many endpoints

LAZY + EntityGraph/JOIN FETCH:
- load relation only for methods that need it
- better control & performance

────────────────────────────────────────
⚠️ LAZY alone is NOT the fix

LAZY stops automatic loading.
If your code/API still touches the collection in a loop → N+1 still happens.
Fix = fetch strategy for that use case (join/graph/dto/batch).

────────────────────────────────────────
Detect it
- enable SQL logging
- see 1 select + many selects by FK
- or use datasource-proxy / p6spy in tests

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary (Roman Urdu)
N+1: pehle parent list (1 query), phir har parent ke liye child query (N).
Default LAZY rakho; jab data chahiye ho tab JOIN FETCH / EntityGraph / DTO use karo.

Interview one-liner:
“N+1 is one query for parents plus one per child relation access. Keep LAZY defaults and fetch intentionally with join fetch, entity graphs, or DTO projections.”
